; SELPH depth filter — controls which components are tried at each depth
;
; This is a SELPH program that the synthesizer calls once per (component, depth)
; to decide whether to include it.  By expressing pruning as a SELPH program,
; the meta-curriculum can synthesize better filters.
;
; Input: namespace with keys:
;   "name"     — component name (string)
;   "priority" — priority score from interleaved learning (number)
;   "arity"    — number of parameters (number)
;   "ret-type" — return type tag: 0=num, 1=str, 2=bool (number)
;   "depth"    — current synthesis depth level (number)
;
; Output: boolean (true = try this component, false = skip it)

; Priority-based depth filter:
; At depth 1, try everything (we need the full pool).
; At depth 2+, only try components with priority above a threshold.
; This prevents low-priority components from exploding the search space
; at higher depths where combinations grow quadratically.
;
; The threshold increases with depth:
;   depth 1: all components (threshold = -1, everything passes)
;   depth 2: priority >= 10 (skip never-used builtins)
;   depth 3: priority >= 25 (only well-established components)
;
; Components with arity 0 (constants) are always included — they're cheap.
; The "x" variable (priority 100) always passes.

(lambda (ctx)
  (let ((depth (ns-get ctx "depth"))
        (priority (ns-get ctx "priority"))
        (arity (ns-get ctx "arity")))
    (if (= arity 0)
      true                                    ; constants always included
      (if (= depth 1)
        true                                  ; depth 1: try everything
        (if (= depth 2)
          (> priority 10)                     ; depth 2: skip low-priority
          (> priority 25))))))                ; depth 3+: only high-priority
